try it out there.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.950
And lastly, the
solutions.rstudio.com website

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.620
will be hosting a lot
of examples I use today.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.285
And that was intentional
in terms of--

00:01:32.285 --> 00:01:34.160
I want you to be able
to see some of the code

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.940
that I'm using so that you can
understand just how quickly you

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.140
could go from using
something locally

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.470
to putting it out there.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.650
So again, RStudio Connect--

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.530
what it is is a content
hub and execution engine

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:51.690
for all your data products.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:55.970
So this could be things like web
apps, machine learning models,

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:59.420
as RESTful APIs,
dashboards, reports,

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.310
automatic emails or
custom emails, tables,

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.680
and visualizations.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.440
Basically, anything you're
already creating in R

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.630
it makes it where you
can take those insights

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.970
and leverage them a
little bit faster.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:16.615
So you can share it
widely, share it securely,

00:02:16.615 --> 00:02:17.990
get it off your
local environment

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:22.230
and put it out there for your
organization to actually use.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.690
And importantly, RStudio
Connect is fully interoperable.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:27.300
So it will play
with any of the data

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.640
products you can make in
R. And additionally, it

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.310
can communicate with
things like R and Python

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.310
or R and Spark, Javascript,
Tensorflow, SQL.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.130
All these things can be
integrated into the data

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.550
products you're creating.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:42.600
So you have a nice platform to
push out all of these things

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.230
that you're making.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.230
The basic idea of what it would
look like in your environment,

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:50.060
and this is just a
very simple example,

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.430
is you create something
locally, maybe

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:56.090
on your desktop in RStudio IDE
or RStudio Server or RStudio

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.470
Server Pro.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:58.970
You then have
push-button deployment

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.450
to push whatever you create,
say a Shiny application or R

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.830
Markdown document, onto Connect.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.160
Connect will then host it.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.870
It will provide all the
scaling and security you need.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.180
It can connect to your
file share system.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.370
It could connect
to your databases.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.500
And ultimately your
end users will just

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.230
access that content via
their own web browser.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.480
So they really just
see it as a website.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.530
But in reality it's a
hosted, on premise solution

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:25.190
for your group.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.290
It could also be
hosted on the Cloud.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.350
But the idea being that you
install the software wherever

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.750
you want it to be, and you
have full access and control

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:36.030
over its own security.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.120
Now this being said, this is
a relatively simple example.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.670
And when people start
talking about production,

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.740
you start thinking of
these massive environments.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.970
Whether it's thousands
of users, or lots

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.850
of people hitting your
API calls, or just

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.450
doing things at scale.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:56.400
And so for a lot of people
production is this buzzword.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.430
Or this idea of putting
things into production

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.540
is scary for a lot of people.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.110
And so I want to go
over how at least I

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.970
think about production
environments

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.280
and how we approach
this with Connect.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.457
And how you can start
where you are-- maybe

00:04:10.457 --> 00:04:12.540
you're more advanced, maybe
you're less advanced--

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:16.070
and grow within Connect.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.079
So, what does
production mean to me?

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:19.610
I'll steal a quote
from Joe Cheng

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:22.863
that he gave at the
most recent RStudio Con.

00:04:22.863 --> 00:04:24.530
"Production means a
software environment

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.530
that is used and then
relied upon by real users,

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.810
with real consequences
if things go wrong."

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.728
So specifically, this
doesn't talk about how big

00:04:33.728 --> 00:04:34.520
the environment is.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.830
It doesn't talk about
how widely it's used.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.110
Just something the
business relies upon,

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.660
something the business is
using to either make decisions

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.795
or to gain insight about
some data science product.

00:04:44.795 --> 00:04:46.910
And that's really the
ultimate goal data science--

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.970
is to inform a business
decision to provide

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.430
value of some type
for the organization

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:55.040
or for the individual.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.620
Beyond that basic
idea, in my mind

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.120
there's some other
production needs.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.220
So things like
scaling and execution.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.960
You want to have
it be able to work

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.510
for your entire organization
if you wanted to, as opposed

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.810
to just locally on
your own laptop.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.688
You want to have security
and access control.

00:05:10.688 --> 00:05:12.980
So you don't want to just
put it out there for everyone

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.660
on the internet to access.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.850
And even within your
own group, maybe you

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:21.080
want to have only Execs see
these specific materials

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.960
or only your data science
group see other material.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.870
You also want to have
logging and visibility

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:28.610
into the applications
you've already

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.730
pushed out there or the things
you're using in production.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.800
As well as logging
about the performance

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:38.140
or when they've been updated
or changes that have been made.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.020
And as Garrett covered in the
last webinar, reproducibility

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.490
is also a huge component.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:45.730
And with a code based
solution like R,

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.910
you're really focusing on
being able to reproduce

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.850
both that environment, as well
as the actual code that you

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.600
used to create your asset.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.600
And lastly something I
think is really important

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.120
is the sharing and
collaboration idea.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.410
So sharing in terms of--
you're creating something.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.360
You spent a lot of time
on creating a model

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.050
or evaluating a model, making
some type of prediction,

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.000
doing some core
data science task.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.760
And then ultimately you have
to inform somebody, or share

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:17.208
that with somebody, and have
a useful discussion with them.

00:06:17.208 --> 00:06:19.500
Additionally, you want to
have collaboration with them.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.400
So maybe you have to send
reports back and forth,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.350
or collaborate back
and forth with someone

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.680
who doesn't use R. But
they're making big decisions,

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.390
but they may not be a coder.

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:31.310
So being able to bring
them into the fold

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:34.070
and give them an asset
that they can work with.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.380
In the last part of that
sharing collaboration aspect

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:37.760
is discoverability.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.270
So being able to
go onto your area

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:44.120
and find things of interest
that may be useful for your team

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.790
to make decisions.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:48.830
Ultimately, the idea
here is-- production

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:52.610
doesn't have to be as scary
as it always comes out to be.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:54.380
In So yes, there are
some environments

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.660
that are extremely locked
down, extremely complex.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:59.090
They're serving thousands
and thousands of users.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:03.082
And a lot goes into it
from a deadlock standpoint.

00:07:03.082 --> 00:07:04.790
There's other people
who really just want

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.100
to take some type
of R-based product

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:10.130
and put it out there
for 5 to 10 people.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:12.800
And the concept here is
that I have customers

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.300
on both ends of the spectrum.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.480
Very small groups serving other
small groups, and other people

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:20.660
who are serving hundreds
or thousands of individuals

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:21.860
within their organization.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.470
And that works for
both of these groups.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.650
And as well as the
spectrum across them.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.640
So in terms of what
Connect provides in

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:35.580
this concept is hitting on the
same points that I said before.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.570
So scaling, execution,
high availability.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:40.685
Basically, you can take
your Shiny application

00:07:40.685 --> 00:07:44.100
or your R Markdown down and
scale that to organization.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.240
Additionally, because Connect
can actually execute R code,

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:50.900
it can actually really run
your R Scripts or R Markdown

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:55.520
documents or your Shiny
applications for new people.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.470
As far as the high
availability concept,

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:00.060
you can have multiple
Connect servers in a cluster.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.480
So if part of your
server were to go down,

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:04.490
you have another
one ready to go.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:07.220
Or if you just want to share the
load across multiple servers,

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.500
that's also possible.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:11.840
Additionally, you have full
security access control

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:12.650
and visibility.

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:18.080
So you can bring your own LDAP,
Active Directory, SAML security

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:19.940
to authenticate users.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:21.260
You have access control.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:24.410
So at the application
or server level

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:27.290
you can specify
who can see what.

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:29.420
As well as have
applications that

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:33.510
are only for testing or
only for production use.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.020
And lastly you have full
visibility in terms of-- you

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.890
can see what's been
published there.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.590
And you can understand
what's being

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:41.621
used in your organization.

00:08:41.621 --> 00:08:43.204
Additionally, from
the reproducibility

00:08:43.204 --> 00:08:44.282
and logging standpoint--

00:08:44.282 --> 00:08:45.740
I'll cover this a
little bit later.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:49.130
But you have these sandbox
environments on Connect

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.370
where you publish something,
it tracks the R environment,

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:55.020
and tracks all the different
packages that are being used.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:59.090
So you have this metadata about
the assets you're creating.

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:02.960
You also have logging about
both use of it and updates

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.430
to those assets.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:07.280
And lastly you have full
sharing and collaboration.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.830
So yes, you can now take
in R Markdown document

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:12.620
and just publish the finished
document out on the Connect

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:15.030
and then people can
access it via web browser,

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:17.060
which is a huge win
for a lot of people.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:20.060
But additionally, it opens
up as an execution engine

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:22.580
where those people who
may not know how to code

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:25.820
can actually re-execute those
R Markdown documents or Shiny

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:29.000
applications to create
something they want to see,

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.730
without ever having
to go and bother

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:33.858
the data scientist directly.

00:09:33.858 --> 00:09:35.400
And lastly you have
the collaboration

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:36.233
and discoverability.

00:09:36.233 --> 00:09:38.150
You can actually
search around R Connect

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:40.580
and find things of interest.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.130
Ultimately, Connect's
getting your data products

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:45.380
off your laptop and
ready for consumption

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:48.628
in your environment.

00:09:48.628 --> 00:09:50.170
Again, when I talked
about security--

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.480
you have three major
levels of security

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:53.630
that I'd like to focus on.

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:56.500
So your server level,
just authenticated users

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:57.740
accessing your content.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.035
So you can bring
your own security.

00:10:00.035 --> 00:10:02.160
A lot of people are familiar
with Active Directory,

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:03.277
LDAP, SAML.

00:10:03.277 --> 00:10:05.110
There's some other ones
there, but those are

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:07.270
the three big players we see.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:08.590
You also have app level.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:12.080
So you can restrict user
access to your applications,

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:14.380
whether they're
Shiny or R Markdown.

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:16.780
Kind of going in there
and filtering out

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.010
who needs to see what.

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:22.090
And lastly, you even have data
level access to specific things

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:23.600
even within an application.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:26.590
So all these different
tiers of security and access

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:31.310
that you like to see in
your production environment.

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:33.350
As far as the
reproducibility, I hit on this

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:34.550
a little bit earlier.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:37.413
But the big idea here
is that you really

00:10:37.413 --> 00:10:39.830
don't want to create something,
put it out there, and then

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.650
all of a sudden not have
governance or understanding

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:44.900
of how you created it.

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:47.210
So you don't want to lose
the code, you don't want

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:50.150
to lose the information
about which package version

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:52.790
you were on, or which
version of R you were on,

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:55.280
or what were even the
system dependencies

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.980
that you were using there.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:01.430
So importantly, the way
that Connect works is you

00:11:01.430 --> 00:11:03.230
create an asset locally.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.900
So maybe it's on a server,
through RStudio Server,

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.325
or on the desktop IDE.

00:11:08.325 --> 00:11:09.950
So you write an
application, and you're

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:11.210
creating R Markdown document.

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:14.390
Whatever you're doing, you
actually bundle that together

00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:15.530
when you click Publish.

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:17.330
So this is all
done automatically,

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:19.310
you just click
Publish, Connect will

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.350
bundle all that
information together

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:23.700
so you have the source
code, you have the metadata

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:25.490
around a specific dependencies.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:27.230
That's pushed out to Connect.

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:30.800
Connect then unpacks the bundle,
with this packrat bundle.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.530
And it actually re-installs
the specific dependencies

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:36.000
at the exact package
version you were using.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.210
So the same version
of R, the same version

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:40.260
of the exact packages
you were using.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:42.740
So you have this
sandbox environment

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:45.110
of exactly what
you want it to be.

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:48.390
Importantly, each data product
has its own R environment.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:51.260
So this prevents breaking
existing deployed content.

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.260
You can have multiple versions
of R basically running

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:55.640
across your different assets.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:57.350
Multiple different
package versions

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:00.578
running across your different
assets, if you wanted to.

00:12:00.578 --> 00:12:02.120
We have a little
bit more information

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:05.150
here in the documentation,
in the admin

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:07.010
guide about package management.

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:09.620
And if you'd like to know
more about package manager,

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:11.431
we can also have
that discussion.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:17.690
The next step I'd like to
cover is the deployment aspect.

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:20.710
So when people talk
about "pushing to prod."

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:23.170
The joke is always,
don't push anything out

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:24.700
on Friday afternoon,
you don't want

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.250
to just put something
out there into the world

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:28.730
and then disappear
for the weekend.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:31.390
So people will think
very differently

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:33.730
about how they're
publishing or how

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:37.120
they're deploying assets into
their production environment.

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:38.960
For a group just
starting out, they're

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:41.260
ecstatic and super
excited about just

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:42.550
being pushed by deployment.

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:45.670
Just being able to create
something locally and basically

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:48.460
in a few seconds publish it
out and had it ready to go

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:50.830
and shared across
the entire group.

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:53.812
Other groups are very much
more about the governance

00:12:53.812 --> 00:12:55.270
in the DevOps side,
where they want

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:58.960
to have man in the middle
or woman in the middle.

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:01.908
Just someone there who is
making a decision about

00:13:01.908 --> 00:13:03.325
is this ready to
go to production?

00:13:03.325 --> 00:13:06.940
So if that means code review,
or putting it into Git,

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:08.840
or continuous integration.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:12.600
So I'll cover all
these options briefly.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:15.100
The first option would be just
push-button deployment, which

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:17.683
is the default.
So within the IDE

00:13:17.683 --> 00:13:19.600
you can actually click
this little blue button

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.450
and it will publish
things onto Connect.

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:24.820
At that point you could
still restrict access.

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:27.100
So maybe you want to
push it out, play around

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.310
with it on Connect, and only
you can access it at this point.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:33.080
And then after the
fact, you could say,

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:36.190
OK, well this is running
well I'll allow access

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:37.450
to the rest of our audience.

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:40.270
And open it up to
the rest of the group

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:44.030
to use the data product.

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:46.400
Alternatively, you could
put things out through Git.

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:48.300
So you could have a
Git-backed deployment

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:50.550
where you're not getting
pushed by deployment anymore.

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:52.850
But rather you're actually
pushing something out

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:54.380
in a version
control through Git.

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:56.600
And then Connect is
calling out to Git

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:59.100
and installing it
directly from there.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:00.840
So in this case
you publish it out,

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:03.680
RStudio Connect will
publish the initial version.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:05.210
And then will actually update--

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:08.705
pulling different Git
updates onto Connect itself.

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:14.630
On the alternative side, rather
than having this one step

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:16.730
process, you could
have a testing and then

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:17.930
a production environment.

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:19.970
Or what we are going to
call a staging and then

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:21.450
a production environment.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:24.200
So this case, you actually
publish your asset initially

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:27.940
into a sandbox staging
or testing environment.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:29.600
So a different
version of Connect.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:32.600
And only your DevOps
or data science team

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:33.950
is accessing at this point.

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:36.740
Or just dog fooding it,
testing it, making sure things

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.150
are rock solid.

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:39.560
Once it's gone
through that stage

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.380
you can then re-deploy it onto
your production environment

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:44.660
and let the rest of
your consumers use it.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:47.588
Now that it's working properly.

00:14:47.588 --> 00:14:49.380
So this would be a
little bit more complex.

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:51.290
But in reality, you're
still able to use

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:55.150
the same push-button
deployment here.

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:56.670
Alternatively, you
get the same idea

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:58.587
where you're pushing
things out into a sandbox

00:14:58.587 --> 00:15:00.570
doing your user
approval and testing,

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:02.200
but after this you
actually will ask

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:03.725
IT to publish its production.

00:15:03.725 --> 00:15:05.850
So now you're bringing in
an additional stakeholder

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:07.517
because you want to
have more governance

00:15:07.517 --> 00:15:10.200
or you want to have more
insight into bringing

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:12.570
the rest of the
administration team here.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:15.650
And they will actually then
publish it into production.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:17.860
All these are valid ideas.

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:20.610
But the concept here
I'm trying to cover

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:22.620
is that Connect
is ready to serve

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:26.490
all these different
environments or approaches.

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:29.200
And lastly, another one would
be continuous integration.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:32.850
So still doing your sandboxing,
your testing approval.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.350
And then the request
is actually set

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:37.460
to merge into your
version control.

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:39.330
It goes to code
review, it's pushed out

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:42.540
to the master branch, and then
a continuous integration server

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:45.010
will actually publish
its production itself.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.580
So something like Jenkins
will go out there,

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:49.890
pull the that's been updated,
and then republished it

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:51.990
onto Connect.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:54.210
Going through this
relatively quickly.

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:56.850
Just again, the
idea that wherever

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:58.800
you are in your data
science journey,

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:00.810
Connect is ready to
meet you at that point.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:07.610
Getting more off of
the administration side

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.370
and quickly into the
data product side.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:13.700
Again I mentioned I'd be
covering a lot of R Markdown.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.220
A lot of people are
familiar with R Markdown,

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:19.460
on their local environment,
writing reports, maybe creating

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:23.870
a blog or even creating
small websites.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:25.520
And the idea being
that you can create

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.937
all these things but then where
are you going to put them?

00:16:27.937 --> 00:16:29.990
And Connect can be that place.

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:31.520
Obviously you can
also host things

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:33.200
like Shiny
applications, which has

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:34.850
been covered in other webinars.

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:36.800
Or even putting a
model onto production

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:40.190
as a RESTful API, which has also
been covered in another link

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:43.250
that I shared in this webinar.

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:46.070
But the idea here
is that you have

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:49.670
a spectrum of interactivity
with your documents.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:51.680
And Garret covered this
really well last week.

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:54.290
So I stole this site from him.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:58.130
The idea of, you have this
spectrum of more interactivity.

00:16:58.130 --> 00:17:01.950
So a local .Rmd that you're
working in interactively.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:04.100
And then you knit it once,
and so it's not really

00:17:04.100 --> 00:17:05.480
that interactive.

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:07.595
Maybe you have an
.Rmd with parameters.

00:17:07.595 --> 00:17:12.020
So now can create many reports
from a single master report.

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:15.680
You could add an HTMLwidgets
for front-end interactivity that

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:17.760
doesn't require
a server back-end

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:22.760
and gives you all this fancy
Shiny-esque environment

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:24.560
or interactivity.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:27.530
But you don't have to have it
hosted with a Shiny back-end.

00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:30.500
And then you could fully put
a Shiny component onto it.

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:33.170
So have a flexdashboard
or a R Markdown

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:35.390
based source with Shiny runtime.

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:38.300
You've got a server in the
back-end that gives you

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:39.680
further interactivity.

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:42.230
And then all the way out to a
Shiny application which could

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:43.680
be a truly custom web app.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.160
So not necessarily a
report or a dashboard,

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:50.470
but truly something that
you just created yourself.

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:52.030
Now, the idea being
that I'm going

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:55.180
to focus on the first
three points here--

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:57.400
basic R Markdowns, R
Markdowns with parameters,

00:17:57.400 --> 00:17:59.290
and R Markdowns
with HTMLwidgets.

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:03.580
Specifically on RStudio Connect.

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:05.950
And this is why I like to
think about it as R Markdown

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:07.060
and production.

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:11.620
Not just using R Markdown
as your basic reporting

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:12.860
just for yourself.

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:16.600
But thinking of R Markdown as a
key player in your data science

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:17.170
environment.

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:19.960
And in your data science kind
of production environment

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:25.660
where you're using R
Markdown as a way to create

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:29.470
repeated jobs, or reports,
or automated emails.

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:31.780
All these different things
that can provide value

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:36.250
for your company that you
may not be using today.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.443
So the basic idea of
an R Markdown report--

00:18:38.443 --> 00:18:40.110
again I'll just cover
it briefly in case

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:42.030
you missed last week's webinar.

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:45.000
It's an open source R package
that produces high production

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:49.180
quality documents by integrating
prose with code and results.

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:51.990
Basically, you can write,
you can create code,

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:55.330
and you can create outputs
all within a single document.

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:58.380
So this is really useful for
data scientists who are already

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:00.990
using R and they want
to both create a report

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:03.930
and share the report
in a reproducible way.

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:06.450
It can create all sorts of
different output formats.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:10.410
Everything from basic
documents, PDF, journals,

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:15.480
full-blown web pages, Word
docs, other Markdown formats.

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:19.020
You can do presentations,
whether they're outputs PDF

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:21.600
or is an HTML output.

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:24.120
Dashboards through
flexdashboards.

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:29.400
Full websites, or blogs, or
knowledge repos, and then

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:31.320
also full books.

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:33.570
Importantly, all of these
different output formats

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.240
can actually be published
and hosted on Connect.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:38.460
So you can actually publish
the finished product.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:42.390
As well as bundle the code
and the finished product

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:44.130
where it can actually
be re-executed

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:46.500
on Connect without having
to write additional code.

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:50.700
Get to the next one.

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:51.900
There we go.

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:55.310
So with Parameterized
Reports, taking the next step

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:56.930
from R Markdown.

00:19:56.930 --> 00:20:00.830
The idea here is that you
take a single R Markdown,

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:02.660
write a function
around it, and then you

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:06.590
can loop through or use
a map function from purrr

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:08.850
to create multiple
versions of this report.

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:11.420
So this is just a fake
report I put together.

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:15.920
The idea here is that your
rendering a specific R Markdown

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:16.880
document.

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:19.970
You're listing some
parameters, so these parameters

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:22.040
tell you that state
and year for it

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:26.730
will be generated on the
fly based upon your inputs.

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:31.170
And then you're creating this
output file as an HTML output.

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:32.960
So you write this function.

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:35.380
You specify your input.

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:38.960
So there's four different inputs
here, four states repeated,

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:40.370
and then multiple years.

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:42.770
And then you can knit
this report locally

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:43.995
four different times.

00:20:43.995 --> 00:20:45.620
So this is really
powerful in terms of,

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:48.230
you can create multiple reports
on your local environment

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:51.090
really quickly based
off the same code.

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:52.850
The next step with
RStudio Connect

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:55.340
is you can take the same
parameterized report,

00:20:55.340 --> 00:20:57.560
publish onto Connect
with the source code.

00:20:57.560 --> 00:20:59.565
And someone who doesn't
have R installed

00:20:59.565 --> 00:21:01.190
on their local machine,
or doesn't even

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:04.340
know how to code in R,
and they're actually

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:08.180
able to have a user interface
to basically re-knit this report

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:09.840
with parameters of interest.

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:14.650
So you take the same parameters,
and put a front-end on it.

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:16.620
You can also use things
like HTML widgets,

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:19.080
which I mentioned before
as front-end interactivity.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:21.407
So those do not require
a server back-end.

00:21:21.407 --> 00:21:23.490
So you could publish R
Markdown document with, say

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:27.030
the leaflet package, which
provides a JavaScript

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:28.680
and HTML on the front-end.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:31.830
We're able to interact and
get this Google Maps-esque,

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:34.350
but it's really
an OpenStreetMap,

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:36.900
interactivity around on maps.

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:39.360
There's a ton of
different HTMLwidgets.

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:42.180
And even things like Crosstalk,
where you can have HTMLwidgets

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:44.790
talk to each other
on the same web page,

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:45.990
even without a server.

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:47.865
You could always add a
server on the back-end

00:21:47.865 --> 00:21:49.350
with Shiny runtime.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:53.250
But these three links here will
cover additional HTMLwidgets

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:56.160
that I won't get to today.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:58.610
And lastly, something of
interest and something that

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:01.680
I'm really excited about
is the Blastula package,

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:07.590
which is a way of creating
beautiful HTML emails in R

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:08.930
and in .Rmds.

00:22:08.930 --> 00:22:10.370
So the basic idea
is that you can

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:14.570
write a basic script
or almost like an R

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:18.560
Markdown-esque format here of
what you want the email to say.

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:22.790
It can then run this email
based off of some R input.

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:26.540
And have an output of
this beautiful HTML

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:28.340
email that can
then be pushed out

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:30.240
through Connect automatically.

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:31.790
So yes, you could
create a report

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:33.210
that people can interact with.

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:35.360
You could also have
that same report

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:38.630
copy-pasted into an email and
sent to a specific person,

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:41.990
even if they're not
accessing Connect.

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:43.550
And so these three
things here are

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:46.400
something I'm going to cover
on the demo portion, which I'll

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:48.050
get over to here in a second.

00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:50.660
And lastly, there
is this link here

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:52.500
where Kelly O'Brien
actually put together

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:55.190
an example of customs
scheduled emails with Blastula

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:56.250
and RStudio Connect.

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:59.280
And it's a great read through.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:02.130
With this I'd like to jump over
to the demonstration point.

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:06.170
So let me switch screens.

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:09.260
Zoom in a little bit.

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:13.220
All right, so, for most people
hopefully this is familiar.

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:17.690
In terms of, this
is the RStudio IDE.

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:19.490
Importantly, I'm
actually accessing this

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:20.610
through my web browser.

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:23.690
So this is RStudio Server Pro.

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:26.300
You could technically
use the desktop IDE

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:29.390
and get the same
experience with publishing.

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:31.640
So the idea here is that
I create an application.

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:36.110
If I want to publish it, I can
publish it to Connect here.

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:38.810
By just clicking this
blue Publish button,

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:41.210
I take all the source code--

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:43.640
it finds the data that
is necessary to run

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:46.642
this, bundles it all together
and says, do you to publish it?

00:23:46.642 --> 00:23:48.350
Now I've actually
published this already,

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:50.820
so I be updating
the existing asset.

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:53.240
But I would just click Publish,
and it would push all that

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:55.940
and reinstall the packages
onto Colorado, which

00:23:55.940 --> 00:23:59.780
is our internal connect server.

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:02.900
But the idea here
is that, that's it.

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:04.215
That's push-button deployment.

00:24:04.215 --> 00:24:05.840
One button pressed,
you're ready to go,

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:07.880
and it's pushed out there.

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:10.710
This example here is
a Shiny application.

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:13.820
This could be R
Markdown for HTML.

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:17.840
This could be R Markdown
for a PDF document.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:21.170
Or it could be even a
xaringan Mood Reader

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:28.460
or xaringan for a presentation
with HTML CSS on the front-end.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:31.190
So this application-- we'll just
go through the Shiny App first.

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:32.990
I published it onto Connect.

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:35.670
The basic landing page
for Connect gets me here.

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:37.400
So the immediate
thing of interest

00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:40.010
is that you had your
discoverability built in.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:42.290
I can see all the content I own.

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:44.510
I could also filter
back out and just

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:47.420
look at all the server content.

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:50.030
Going in and even searching
for, maybe I just want

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:53.570
to look for PDF, I could
search for PDF documents

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:56.025
and find things that
have been knit in PDF.

00:24:56.025 --> 00:24:58.370
I also have a full-blown
tagging system.

00:24:58.370 --> 00:25:01.100
So internally, we do
this around technologies.

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:03.950
But you could imagine a world
where, in your environment,

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:06.710
you actually want to filter
it out by specific teams

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:08.575
or by specific assets.

00:25:08.575 --> 00:25:09.950
But the idea being
that you could

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:14.240
filter at many different
levels and find content that

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:15.260
is meaningful to you.

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:20.160
Additionally from an
administrative side,

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:21.660
you can see all the
different people

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:23.520
who have access to content.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:24.960
You can see the different roles.

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:27.450
So publishers versus
viewers, so people

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:30.180
who can only consume that
content versus people

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:31.290
who publish.

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:32.910
All the way down
to administrators

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:36.720
who have additional privileges
around changing assets

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:38.580
that have already
been published.

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:41.760
I can even go in here and
lock or edit the administrator

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:43.360
roles for this person.

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:49.950
So from an access perspective,
you have full control for this.

00:25:49.950 --> 00:25:53.780
From, not having to
go into a config file.

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:57.080
You also have visibility into
the use of your applications

00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:58.560
or the use of your server.

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:02.210
You can see how many people
are actually on your server.

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:04.160
How many connections
are being used.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:07.080
How much RAM and CPU of
your server is being used.

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:08.450
Is it getting hit a lot?

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:12.800
Or is it being used
at a low level?

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:14.375
You also have your audit logs.

00:26:14.375 --> 00:26:16.250
You can actually see
all the different things

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:18.270
that have happened,
different events over time.

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:20.180
So you have this
visibility into things that

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:22.880
are occurring on your server.

00:26:22.880 --> 00:26:25.260
With that, we have
about 15 minutes left.

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:27.920
So I do want to get
into the demo portion.

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.110
The first idea here is--

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:32.130
I've taken my existing code.

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:35.630
So this Access-to-care
Hospital Dashboard,

00:26:35.630 --> 00:26:38.000
which was created by
Edgar-- one of the solutions

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:39.980
engineers here at RStudio.

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.363
And this has been
published onto Connect.

00:26:42.363 --> 00:26:44.030
There's a couple of
things going on here

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:45.030
that I want to show you.

00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:48.560
In terms of, this is actually
a flexdashboard setup.

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:51.830
So you would have a Shiny
runtime on top of that.

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:53.330
So because it has
a Shiny runtime,

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:56.510
that means you can
re-execute R code on the fly.

00:26:56.510 --> 00:26:59.840
So I can switch between states,
and it will re-execute R code

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:01.880
and give me some
type of new output.

00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:04.640
On the front-end, I actually
have an HTML widget.

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:07.010
So this interactivity
here, doesn't

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:08.430
require the server back-end.

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:10.790
So this could be shifted
over into a pure R Markdown

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:12.680
document.

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:17.520
I'm focusing on this example
because it's a simple example.

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:20.060
The basic idea is,
how many hospitals

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.260
do you need per population?

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:24.890
And you can see that
with our linear model

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:30.170
here, about 80.78 RSquared.

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:32.490
The idea here is that as
your population increases,

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:33.500
you need more hospitals.

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:36.140
So I'm going to be using this
example for the rest of my R

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:37.070
Markdown examples.

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:40.870
But wanted to start
with the Shiny example.

00:27:40.870 --> 00:27:43.360
Because this is
Shiny, you have access

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:46.730
to the Shiny runtime in
terms of the server itself.

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:49.990
So you could go in here and
specify specific settings,

00:27:49.990 --> 00:27:52.120
or you just use
a server default,

00:27:52.120 --> 00:27:54.220
about how to scale
up this application

00:27:54.220 --> 00:27:55.960
or how performant
you want to be.

00:27:55.960 --> 00:28:00.423
So maybe you want it to idle out
and close down pretty quickly.

00:28:00.423 --> 00:28:01.840
Because once people
stop using it,

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:05.030
you want it to free up
compute on your server.

00:28:05.030 --> 00:28:07.300
So you could go in here
for the Shiny portion,

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:10.480
and change all these
different factors.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:12.310
But as I mentioned
before, we really

00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:13.607
want to focus on R Markdown.

00:28:13.607 --> 00:28:15.940
But I just wanted to show you
that you have this ability

00:28:15.940 --> 00:28:18.430
to change the runtime and
when you cook at a runtime,

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:20.680
if it's a Shiny application,
it has these options.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:27.090
So I mentioned a little
bit about HTML widgets.

00:28:27.090 --> 00:28:29.610
And I want to show you
this specific example that

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:32.040
is a "Hello, World"
more basic example

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:34.620
of HTML widgets plus Crosstalk.

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:37.770
So in this case, there
isn't a Shiny runtime.

00:28:37.770 --> 00:28:39.750
So there's no server
on the back-end,

00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:42.310
this is just the HTML front-end.

00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:46.260
So in this case, I can actually
filter and change my graph

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:49.710
here without having
to re-run R code.

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:51.175
I published this out.

00:28:51.175 --> 00:28:52.800
And the only thing
that's being changed

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:55.800
is HTML and JavaScript
on the front-end.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.770
So all this interactivity
I have built in

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:02.400
doesn't require a
running Shiny example.

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:06.330
And you can even click here
and filter into small examples

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:09.990
of filtering the graph itself.

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:12.110
So basically the idea
here that I'm setting up

00:29:12.110 --> 00:29:15.830
is that you're R Markdown could
be built as a Shiny component

00:29:15.830 --> 00:29:17.660
or you could build
it out here with just

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:21.040
front-end interactivity.

00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:23.890
So going back to out Access
to Care by State example.

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:26.230
Maybe you take that Shiny
application and you're like,

00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:28.720
oh well, I really just
want to send out reports

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:31.480
to each of the state governors.

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:35.020
And say, hey, your state is
doing well or doing poorly,

00:29:35.020 --> 00:29:39.640
and how many hospitals it
has for user of population.

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:42.850
So you can take that same
R Markdown based report,

00:29:42.850 --> 00:29:45.990
change the output to PDF,
and publish this the Connect

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:46.510
as well.

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:49.710
So now it's hosted, it's
got authenticated access,

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:51.510
and only people who
are allowed to see it

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:53.400
can actually come in
here and access it.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:54.840
You still have the table.

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:56.070
You still have a map.

00:29:56.070 --> 00:29:58.260
Because it's PDF
there's no interactivity

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:00.660
it's just a static document.

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:03.990
Importantly, if I go in here
into the Connect portion,

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:06.810
I can see that the access
of who can actually

00:30:06.810 --> 00:30:07.780
edit this document.

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:10.050
So who can change it.

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:11.220
Only these four people.

00:30:11.220 --> 00:30:13.470
So Edgar could actually push
it out and then the three

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:14.700
collaborators.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:17.400
But I've made it where
anyone can access or view

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:19.830
this document without
login required.

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.020
I could make this to
where users specifically

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:25.890
would have to login, or only
to specific users or groups.

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:29.580
Like say, the Administration
Team or the Exec Team.

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:31.920
All the way down to just the
collaborators and the owner

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:33.860
of the document.

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:36.160
Additionally, because it's
not a Shiny application,

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:37.090
there's no runtime.

00:30:37.090 --> 00:30:41.070
So there's no R code being
executed continuously.

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:43.830
But because it's an
R Markdown document

00:30:43.830 --> 00:30:45.420
I could still
schedule this output

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:49.360
to occur at a specific
time interval.

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:53.250
So I could say, yes, I see this
application or this report.

00:30:53.250 --> 00:30:56.910
I want it to run once
a week for eternity.

00:30:56.910 --> 00:31:00.180
Every week it updates the
data and creates a new report.

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:02.190
So I want to see
that here on Connect.

00:31:02.190 --> 00:31:05.100
So I can schedule the report
to occur every Wednesday,

00:31:05.100 --> 00:31:06.150
like today.

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:09.300
I'll have it run
weekly on Wednesday.

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.010
And I want to publish an output.

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:13.290
I can also send an
email after the update.

00:31:13.290 --> 00:31:15.690
So not only does a publish
it here onto Connect,

00:31:15.690 --> 00:31:18.270
it would actually send it to
all the collaborators, all

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:21.660
the viewers, or whoever
you wanted to go out to.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:23.340
So having this
option of having it

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:25.020
be on Connect or in your inbox.

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:30.570
Additionally, beyond
just the pure,

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:32.520
I just want to see this report.

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:35.070
There was a lot more states
here in the Shiny application

00:31:35.070 --> 00:31:36.960
besides just California.

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:39.870
Because this is a
parameterized R Markdown,

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:42.000
I can actually go in
here on the left tab

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:46.680
and change the parameters within
Connect, re-run the report,

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:49.100
and re-generate the output.

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:53.110
So I could change it to Texas,
Colorado, where I wanted to be.

00:31:53.110 --> 00:31:54.190
Re-run the report.

00:31:54.190 --> 00:31:56.590
And it will re-generate that.

00:31:56.590 --> 00:31:59.620
I'll go into the HTML
widget example to show that.

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:03.160
Because it's a little more
fun to have the interactivity.

00:32:03.160 --> 00:32:06.370
So PDF is great but maybe
we want to still have

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:07.270
some interactivity.

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:10.550
So now, this is still
an R Markdown document.

00:32:10.550 --> 00:32:13.390
So there's no Shiny runtime.

00:32:13.390 --> 00:32:16.810
But if I go in here, I can still
get front-end interactivity.

00:32:16.810 --> 00:32:18.940
So what is the user
population there?

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:22.390
How many hospitals are expected
versus how many do they have?

00:32:22.390 --> 00:32:24.670
I have a data table
down here, in terms of I

00:32:24.670 --> 00:32:27.400
can actually click through
and adjust this data table.

00:32:27.400 --> 00:32:30.970
I could sort by
population, alphabetically,

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:33.700
number of hospitals, all
of this interactivity

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:35.120
that's built in here.

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:38.800
And I don't have to use
computational resources

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:40.060
around the Shiny application.

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:43.070
You can still create a wonderful
Shiny application around this,

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:45.630
but just giving you
this alternative.

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.460
Importantly, you
have the same idea.

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:49.470
You could still schedule
this report to run.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:51.690
You can see I had it where
it's running every Monday.

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:53.640
It actually sends me
an email every Monday

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:57.220
so that I get this
updated report.

00:32:57.220 --> 00:32:59.740
Additionally, you can
save different versions.

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:02.430
So I live in Texas, so maybe
I want to always come back

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:03.570
in here and see Texas.

00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:07.230
So I can actually save a
personal report for myself.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:11.190
Just come in here and use
Texas, or go back to California,

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:14.670
or just go back to the
default that we see before.

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:18.540
And again, because it's
parameterized, I can go in here

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:20.850
and give it to
someone who doesn't

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:23.430
know how to code at all
in R, but wants to see

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:25.740
this report for Delaware.

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:28.740
So they can actually come
in here, run the report,

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:32.160
Connect will execute the
code, re-run the report,

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:33.750
re-generate it, re-knit it.

00:33:33.750 --> 00:33:34.320
All of this.

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:37.140
And put it back up here
into your viewport.

00:33:37.140 --> 00:33:39.990
And this person can
then self-service.

00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:42.870
Provide their own analytics
or create their own reporting.

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:46.230
So you can take your data
science model or your data

00:33:46.230 --> 00:33:48.540
science product that
you've created here,

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:52.110
create a report around it, and
then share that widely within

00:33:52.110 --> 00:33:56.250
your organization including
people who can code or people

00:33:56.250 --> 00:33:58.330
who cannot code.

00:33:58.330 --> 00:33:59.390
So same idea.

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:03.250
It's changed the title,
it's created a new map here

00:34:03.250 --> 00:34:06.220
for Delaware-- a lot less
counties here in Delaware--

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:08.830
and then again, it's got
the same interactivity

00:34:08.830 --> 00:34:12.050
on the data table.

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:16.520
Yeah, so, you've got this
parameterization option.

00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:21.159
So I could save this, but
I'm not interested in that.

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:24.580
I want to keep Texas
as my favorite.

00:34:24.580 --> 00:34:27.489
Additionally, you can take
this similar R Markdown report

00:34:27.489 --> 00:34:30.850
and knit it out to a
xaringan presentation.

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:31.880
So same idea.

00:34:31.880 --> 00:34:35.350
You have a x-y coordinate
