00:34:35.350 --> 00:34:37.000
versus user population.

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:40.489
You can look at the linear
relationship between the two.

00:34:40.489 --> 00:34:42.550
And then actually output
the model around that.

00:34:42.550 --> 00:34:44.758
So maybe this would be more
interesting for your data

00:34:44.758 --> 00:34:47.800
scientists, as opposed
to your executive team.

00:34:47.800 --> 00:34:50.830
But similarly this is built
purely from a R Markdown

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:52.000
report.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:56.170
That's been generated out to
a xaringan, HTML, JavaScript

00:34:56.170 --> 00:34:58.790
presentation.

00:34:58.790 --> 00:35:04.600
It could still be scheduled
to re-run or parameterize as

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:07.370
needed.

00:35:07.370 --> 00:35:09.040
Additionally,
because Connect also

00:35:09.040 --> 00:35:11.110
allows for publishing
from Jupyter notebooks,

00:35:11.110 --> 00:35:14.965
you can take the same data,
run similar ideas in terms

00:35:14.965 --> 00:35:18.840
of you're looking at
population versus county level

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:20.470
and how many
hospitals there are.

00:35:20.470 --> 00:35:22.240
And this is actually
a Jupyter notebook

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:24.370
that's been published
onto RStudio Connect.

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:27.280
So maybe you have a lot
of people working in R,

00:35:27.280 --> 00:35:29.980
maybe a lot of people
working in R and Python.

00:35:29.980 --> 00:35:32.290
Like this is
primarily Python code.

00:35:32.290 --> 00:35:34.660
You can have Python
integrated into R Markdown

00:35:34.660 --> 00:35:35.770
through reticulate.

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:38.080
Or if you actually publish
directly from your Jupyter

00:35:38.080 --> 00:35:41.530
notebook onto Connect
through the Jupyter plugin.

00:35:41.530 --> 00:35:44.770
So really making it
a widely used tool

00:35:44.770 --> 00:35:47.320
regardless of the language
you bring to the table.

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:52.480
Moving a little bit
beyond the concept

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:54.370
of all these different reports.

00:35:54.370 --> 00:35:56.200
Maybe doing some things
that would be more

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:58.510
along the lines of a little
bit of data engineering

00:35:58.510 --> 00:36:06.390
or less interesting but very
necessary and useful ideas.

00:36:06.390 --> 00:36:10.580
So here we're doing an extract,
transform, load, or ETL script.

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:12.790
This is run as an R
Markdown document.

00:36:12.790 --> 00:36:16.480
It's running some R code,
generating some type of output,

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:19.960
creating some portfolio
ideas around stock prices.

00:36:19.960 --> 00:36:23.020
And then it's actually
writing this out to a table

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:24.770
within our database.

00:36:24.770 --> 00:36:28.930
So the idea here that you could
take this ETL script as an R

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:30.730
Markdown document
rather than having

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:34.420
to run a cron job or
manually do all this stuff.

00:36:34.420 --> 00:36:36.040
You can just have
it automatically

00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:40.257
update once a week, once a day,
every hour, or once a month.

00:36:40.257 --> 00:36:41.590
However you want to schedule it.

00:36:41.590 --> 00:36:44.950
You could have
this ETL job re-run

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:46.400
and prepare your data for you.

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:48.358
So first thing in the
morning, it's ready to go

00:36:48.358 --> 00:36:50.660
and you don't have to go
in and do this manually.

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:54.730
So again, freeing you up to
do the more intellectually

00:36:54.730 --> 00:36:57.160
rigorous stuff within your
data science environment,

00:36:57.160 --> 00:36:59.590
as opposed to spending a lot
of time re-running things

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:00.090
manually.

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:04.960
Additionally, taking
something still

00:37:04.960 --> 00:37:10.390
within the reporting space
or the R Markdown output,

00:37:10.390 --> 00:37:12.700
creating this
report that's Rich.

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:15.250
And you look at this and
it's a useful report.

00:37:15.250 --> 00:37:17.500
It's got some front-end
interactivity.

00:37:17.500 --> 00:37:20.000
Again, no Shiny here.

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:23.470
